  "sections": [
    {
      "key": "quickFacts",
      "source": "glaser",
      "sourceUrl": "https://www.aquariumglaser.de/en/25-perchlike-fishes/cichlasoma-loisellei-2/",
      "html": "<p>The smallest of the predatory Central American cichlids called \"Guapotes\" (\"the big pretty ones\"). It usually reaches up to 20 cm, exceptionally 25–30 cm (only males). Sexual maturity is reached at about 10 cm.</p>"
    },
    {
      "key": "distribution",
      "source": "glaser",
      "sourceUrl": "https://www.aquariumglaser.de/en/25-perchlike-fishes/cichlasoma-loisellei-2/",
      "html": "<p>In their Central American home countries, the predatory colourful cichlids currently classified in the genus Parachromis (previously Cichlasoma or Nandopsis) are called \"Guapotes\", meaning \"the big pretty ones\".</p>"
    },
    {
      "key": "behaviour",
      "source": "glaser",
      "sourceUrl": "https://www.aquariumglaser.de/en/25-perchlike-fishes/cichlasoma-loisellei-2/",
      "html": "<p>P. loisellei is regarded as the most peaceful species of the genus, in contrast to some other species. It only becomes aggressive if the animals feel molested. A large aquarium is still a basic prerequisite for fish and aquarists to enjoy each other without restriction.</p>"
    },
    {
      "key": "notes",
      "source": "glaser",
      "sourceUrl": "https://www.aquariumglaser.de/en/25-perchlike-fishes/cichlasoma-loisellei-2/",
      "html": "<p>In 2018, a follow-up examination of the type specimen of P. friedrichsthalii, described in 1840, showed that it is identical to P. loisellei, described in 1989. P. loisellei thus became a synonym of P. friedrichsthalii. The fish common in the hobby as P. friedrichsthalii is another species, now called P. multifasciatus.</p>"
    },
    {
      "key": "references",
      "source": "glaser",
      "sourceUrl": "https://www.aquariumglaser.de/en/25-perchlike-fishes/cichlasoma-loisellei-2/",
      "html": "<p>Morgenstern, R. (2018): Fishes collected by Emanuel Ritter von Friedrichsthal in Central America between 1838-1841. Vertebrate Zoology v. 68 (no. 3): 253-267.</p>"
    }
  ],
